1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:00,681
Hello.

2
00:00:00,681 --> 00:00:03,475
This is the technical walkthrough of Unmask.

3
00:00:03,475 --> 00:00:05,063
No screen demo this time:

4
00:00:05,063 --> 00:00:11,603
this is what happens under the hood when you sign in, seal a bid, settle a launch and check the result.

5
00:00:11,603 --> 00:00:16,620
Every value you will see on screen was captured from a real run of the backend.

6
00:00:17,220 --> 00:00:18,912
First, the architecture.

7
00:00:18,912 --> 00:00:23,636
The browser, built with Next.js, does the sealing and the checking.

8
00:00:23,636 --> 00:00:26,385
The backend is Fastify with PostgreSQL.

9
00:00:26,385 --> 00:00:29,910
It stores ciphertext and nothing else about a bid.

10
00:00:29,910 --> 00:00:34,845
A development enclave holds the key, and runs the Rust auction engine.

11
00:00:34,845 --> 00:00:39,075
NEAR is used for sign-in, and NEAR Intents for price quotes.

12
00:00:39,075 --> 00:00:41,472
The dashed boxes are not done yet:

13
00:00:41,472 --> 00:00:45,420
a real enclave with attestation, and real payment locks.

14
00:00:46,020 --> 00:00:48,401
Step one is sign-in, with NEP-413.

15
00:00:48,401 --> 00:00:52,113
The backend issues a one-time challenge with a nonce.

16
00:00:52,113 --> 00:00:53,513
The wallet signs it.

17
00:00:53,513 --> 00:01:01,637
The backend verifies the signature, and then asks the NEAR RPC whether that key is a full-access key on the account.

18
00:01:01,637 --> 00:01:04,438
Only then does it issue a session token.

19
00:01:04,438 --> 00:01:08,360
The challenge is single use and expires in five minutes.

20
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:11,003
Step two is the payment check.

21
00:01:11,003 --> 00:01:13,930
Before bidding, you lock funds for the bid.

22
00:01:13,930 --> 00:01:18,765
The request names the bidder, the token and the amount in atomic units.

23
00:01:18,765 --> 00:01:22,850
Five thousand dollars is five billion units at six decimals.

24
00:01:22,850 --> 00:01:25,097
The response carries a claim key.

25
00:01:25,097 --> 00:01:29,455
That key later goes inside the sealed bid, never to the backend.

26
00:01:29,455 --> 00:01:33,540
Today the check is a database row, and the response says so.

27
00:01:34,140 --> 00:01:37,659
Step three is sealing, and it happens in the browser.

28
00:01:37,659 --> 00:01:39,717
The bid is a small JSON object:

29
00:01:39,717 --> 00:01:45,427
the limit price, the quantity, the payment check and its claim key, and a random salt.

30
00:01:45,427 --> 00:01:51,469
The browser encrypts it with a libsodium sealed box, addressed to the enclave's public key.

31
00:01:51,469 --> 00:01:58,440
A sealed box uses a fresh ephemeral key pair, so only the holder of the enclave's secret key can open it.

32
00:01:58,440 --> 00:02:01,760
Not the site, not the creator, not another bidder.

33
00:02:02,360 --> 00:02:10,807
The commitment is a SHA-256 hash over the launch, the bidder, the limit, the quantity, the payment check and the salt, in a fixed order.

34
00:02:10,807 --> 00:02:12,422
The claim key is left out.

35
00:02:12,422 --> 00:02:16,334
Nobody can guess the amounts from the hash because of the salt.

36
00:02:16,334 --> 00:02:20,620
Later you can show the salt, and prove that this exact bid was yours.

37
00:02:21,220 --> 00:02:22,544
Step four, submit.

38
00:02:22,544 --> 00:02:29,091
The request contains the ciphertext, the commitment, the payment check id and the key id.

39
00:02:29,091 --> 00:02:29,900
No amounts.

40
00:02:29,900 --> 00:02:32,475
The backend answers with a receipt.

41
00:02:32,475 --> 00:02:34,387
This is the row it stores:

42
00:02:34,387 --> 00:02:36,300
ciphertext and commitment.

43
00:02:36,300 --> 00:02:39,389
And this is the public view of the launch:

44
00:02:39,389 --> 00:02:41,449
only a count of sealed bids.

45
00:02:41,449 --> 00:02:46,230
The count is public by design, and it does leak demand over time.

46
00:02:46,830 --> 00:02:49,490
The backend refuses what it should.

47
00:02:49,490 --> 00:02:50,326
No session:

48
00:02:50,326 --> 00:02:51,314
unauthorized.

49
00:02:51,314 --> 00:02:53,366
Raw JSON as the ciphertext:

50
00:02:53,366 --> 00:02:54,431
plaintext bid.

51
00:02:54,431 --> 00:02:57,091
A second bid from the same account:

52
00:02:57,091 --> 00:02:58,155
duplicate bid.

53
00:02:58,155 --> 00:03:01,271
Locking funds for a bid after the window:

54
00:03:01,271 --> 00:03:01,803
closed.

55
00:03:01,803 --> 00:03:03,019
One honest note.

56
00:03:03,019 --> 00:03:09,100
The plaintext guard only checks that the text does not start with a curly brace.

57
00:03:09,100 --> 00:03:13,660
It cannot tell a real sealed box from any other opaque text.

58
00:03:14,260 --> 00:03:15,524
Now the honest part.

59
00:03:15,524 --> 00:03:20,958
In this development setup the backend holds the enclave key, so it can open every bid.

60
00:03:20,958 --> 00:03:25,824
This is the output of the development enclave opening the bid we just sealed.

61
00:03:25,824 --> 00:03:27,973
That is why we call it a stand-in.

62
00:03:27,973 --> 00:03:32,143
The design goal is a key that lives only inside attested hardware.

63
00:03:32,143 --> 00:03:36,630
We have not built that part yet, and the threat model document says so.

64
00:03:37,230 --> 00:03:38,009
Settlement.

65
00:03:38,009 --> 00:03:42,753
Only the creator can call settle, and only after the window closes.

66
00:03:42,753 --> 00:03:44,736
The enclave opens every bid.

67
00:03:44,736 --> 00:03:49,198
It re-checks any agent's rules, and validates payout addresses.

68
00:03:49,198 --> 00:03:52,597
Bids that fail are left out, each with a reason.

69
00:03:52,597 --> 00:03:55,500
Here are the two real ones from this run.

70
00:03:55,500 --> 00:03:57,978
The rest become the engine's input:

71
00:03:57,978 --> 00:04:01,590
canonical strings, with no floating point anywhere.

72
00:04:02,190 --> 00:04:05,104
The engine implements a uniform price rule.

73
00:04:05,104 --> 00:04:07,407
Keep bids at or above the reserve.

74
00:04:07,407 --> 00:04:09,779
Sort by limit price, highest first.

75
00:04:09,779 --> 00:04:13,438
Walk down adding quantity until the supply is reached.

76
00:04:13,438 --> 00:04:16,080
That bid's limit is the clearing price.

77
00:04:16,080 --> 00:04:22,246
Everyone above it is filled in full, and bids exactly at the price share the rest pro-rata.

78
00:04:22,246 --> 00:04:26,447
Every winner pays the clearing price, and money is rounded up.

79
00:04:26,447 --> 00:04:29,090
These are the real fills from this run.

80
00:04:29,690 --> 00:04:32,307
Three hashes make the run checkable.

81
00:04:32,307 --> 00:04:36,523
The input hash is a SHA-256 of the canonical engine input.

82
00:04:36,523 --> 00:04:39,794
The code hash identifies the engine that ran.

83
00:04:39,794 --> 00:04:44,300
The output hash is a SHA-256 of exactly what the engine wrote.

84
00:04:44,300 --> 00:04:47,717
The mode says dev, and the attestation is none.

85
00:04:47,717 --> 00:04:50,697
Anyone can recompute the first and third.

86
00:04:50,697 --> 00:04:54,840
The second is only a claim until a real enclave signs it.

87
00:04:55,440 --> 00:04:58,578
The verify page recomputes six checks in your browser.

88
00:04:58,578 --> 00:04:59,856
Four we can really do:

89
00:04:59,856 --> 00:05:12,754
rebuild the input from the public numbers and match the hash, run the rule again in TypeScript and get the same fills, check that the money adds up to the last unit, and check the published output is what the engine wrote.

90
00:05:12,754 --> 00:05:14,323
Two we cannot do from here:

91
00:05:14,323 --> 00:05:18,100
that the code is the published code, and that hardware signed it.

92
00:05:18,100 --> 00:05:20,540
They show as not checked, never as a pass.

93
00:05:21,140 --> 00:05:23,449
What is real on the chain side.

94
00:05:23,449 --> 00:05:25,014
NEAR sign-in is real:

95
00:05:25,014 --> 00:05:27,174
NEP-413 and an RPC key check.

96
00:05:27,174 --> 00:05:33,059
The NEAR Intents price is a real live quote, a preview with no deposit address.

97
00:05:33,059 --> 00:05:37,306
The engine builds to WebAssembly and runs under wasmtime.

98
00:05:37,306 --> 00:05:42,520
A remote run on OutLayer has not been retried, so nothing is attested.

99
00:05:42,520 --> 00:05:46,990
And there is no token contract and no real payment lock yet.

100
00:05:46,990 --> 00:05:48,480
Those are stand-ins.

101
00:05:49,080 --> 00:05:50,124
How we test it.

102
00:05:50,124 --> 00:05:58,333
The Rust engine and the TypeScript rule agree on two hundred and twelve shared cases, including the WebAssembly build.

103
00:05:58,333 --> 00:06:02,786
Property tests with a seeded generator check the ten guarantees.

104
00:06:02,786 --> 00:06:06,543
Mutation checks confirm the tests catch a broken rule.

105
00:06:06,543 --> 00:06:15,170
There are 133 frontend tests, 64 backend tests, 27 browser tests, and a privacy test with 31 checks on what the API exposes.

106
00:06:15,770 --> 00:06:16,455
To sum up.

107
00:06:16,455 --> 00:06:18,577
Bids are sealed in the browser.

108
00:06:18,577 --> 00:06:22,342
The engine finds one price in exact integer arithmetic.

109
00:06:22,342 --> 00:06:25,559
Anyone can recompute the hashes and the result.

110
00:06:25,559 --> 00:06:30,283
And where we cannot prove something yet, the system says not checked.

111
00:06:30,283 --> 00:06:34,595
The code, the specs and the threat model are in the repository.

112
00:06:34,595 --> 00:06:35,280
Thank you.

